WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:08.180
Socket services need resource governance because peers may be slow, disconnected, malformed, or unexpectedly active.

2
00:00:08.280 --> 00:00:17.080
A service that has no limits can spend too much memory, too many descriptors, too much queue space, or too much time on one peer or one class of requests.

3
00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:28.160
Useful controls include maximum connection counts, maximum message sizes, queue depth limits, request rate limits, idle timeouts, retry limits, and memory caps.

4
00:00:28.260 --> 00:00:32.740
These limits should be chosen for the service's role and enforced consistently.

5
00:00:32.840 --> 00:00:39.400
Limits are most useful when they are part of the protocol and operational documentation, not hidden surprises.

6
00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:46.000
Timeouts and backpressure keep the service from waiting forever or accepting work it cannot process.

7
00:00:46.100 --> 00:00:49.940
A slow peer should not hold critical resources indefinitely.

8
00:00:50.040 --> 00:00:52.840
A full output queue should have a defined response.

9
00:00:52.940 --> 00:00:58.220
A request that exceeds limits should fail predictably and release resources.

10
00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:02.980
Avoid unbounded allocation based on network-provided lengths or counts.

11
00:01:03.080 --> 00:01:08.120
Even when a value is syntactically valid, the service should decide whether it is acceptable.

12
00:01:08.220 --> 00:01:14.140
Allocation, copying, parsing, and dispatch should all happen after size and policy checks.

13
00:01:14.420 --> 00:01:21.060
Cleanup after disconnects, timeouts, parse failures, and protocol errors is part of availability.

14
00:01:21.160 --> 00:01:29.320
Descriptors, buffers, timers, parser state, worker state, and queued output all need a release path.

15
00:01:29.420 --> 00:01:37.180
A long-running service that leaks small amounts of state on failure will eventually become unreliable, and reliability is part of secure socket design.
